1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,903
A ceiling fan has three flat blades.

2
00:00:02,903 --> 00:00:04,998
A seesaw is one straight line.

3
00:00:04,998 --> 00:00:08,891
A sea wall block has four
legs, and one always points up.

4
00:00:09,291 --> 00:00:10,191
Three shapes.

5
00:00:10,092 --> 00:00:11,953
Carbon can make every one of them.

6
00:00:12,403 --> 00:00:13,943
Diya sees it in the kitchen.

7
00:00:13,943 --> 00:00:16,663
She pushes four toothpicks
into a small ball of dough.

8
00:00:17,013 --> 00:00:19,479
She spreads them as far apart as she can.

9
00:00:19,479 --> 00:00:22,415
However it lands, one
toothpick points straight up.

10
00:00:22,915 --> 00:00:25,096
Carbon does the same with its bonds.

11
00:00:25,096 --> 00:00:28,646
By the end, you will know its
shape just by counting bonds.

12
00:00:29,296 --> 00:00:31,206
Welcome to a new chapter.

13
00:00:31,206 --> 00:00:34,898
Organic chemistry is the
chemistry of carbon compounds.

14
00:00:35,248 --> 00:00:37,841
Sugar, cotton, cooking gas, even you.

15
00:00:37,841 --> 00:00:39,241
All built on carbon.

16
00:00:39,591 --> 00:00:42,756
People once thought only
living things could make these.

17
00:00:42,756 --> 00:00:46,706
In eighteen twenty eight, Wohler
made urea in a lab, from a salt.

18
00:00:47,056 --> 00:00:48,848
So what is so special about carbon?

19
00:00:48,848 --> 00:00:50,974
Its electrons are arranged two, then four.

20
00:00:50,974 --> 00:00:52,684
Four electrons in the outer shell.

21
00:00:53,084 --> 00:00:56,695
Each outer electron can pair
with one from another atom.

22
00:00:56,695 --> 00:00:59,692
So carbon makes four
bonds, like four hands.

23
00:01:00,092 --> 00:01:03,131
Having four bonds is
called being tetravalent.

24
00:01:03,131 --> 00:01:04,441
Tetra means four.

25
00:01:05,091 --> 00:01:09,242
Let's see how carbon shapes those
four hands, on our molecule bench.

26
00:01:09,592 --> 00:01:12,615
Carbon starts with two
electrons in 2s and two in 2p.

27
00:01:12,615 --> 00:01:13,965
Only two are unpaired.

28
00:01:14,365 --> 00:01:16,987
One 2s electron jumps up to the empty 2p.

29
00:01:16,987 --> 00:01:18,215
This is promotion.

30
00:01:18,215 --> 00:01:19,683
Now four are unpaired.

31
00:01:20,083 --> 00:01:21,553
Then the orbitals blend.

32
00:01:21,553 --> 00:01:24,701
One s and three p make four
equal s p three orbitals.

33
00:01:25,101 --> 00:01:29,638
They push as far apart as they can,
to the corners of a tetrahedron, at

34
00:01:29,638 --> 00:01:32,215
one hundred and nine point five degrees.

35
00:01:32,215 --> 00:01:34,589
Four single bonds, like in methane.

36
00:01:35,239 --> 00:01:39,489
When carbon makes a double bond,
it mixes one s with only two p.

37
00:01:39,489 --> 00:01:44,126
Three s p two orbitals, flat, at one
hundred and twenty degrees, like in

38
00:01:44,126 --> 00:01:45,026
ethene.

39
00:01:45,165 --> 00:01:46,986
One p orbital is left over.

40
00:01:46,986 --> 00:01:50,088
It stands straight up,
ready for the double bond.

41
00:01:50,488 --> 00:01:53,525
With a triple bond, it
mixes one s with one p.

42
00:01:53,525 --> 00:01:57,919
Two s p orbitals, in a straight line,
at one hundred and eighty degrees,

43
00:01:57,919 --> 00:01:59,002
like in ethyne.

44
00:01:59,002 --> 00:02:00,424
Two p are left over.

45
00:02:00,824 --> 00:02:02,615
So the s share goes up.

46
00:02:02,615 --> 00:02:07,613
Twenty five percent in s p three, about
thirty three in s p two, and fifty

47
00:02:07,613 --> 00:02:08,513
in s p.

48
00:02:08,406 --> 00:02:10,706
This share is called s character.

49
00:02:11,356 --> 00:02:13,295
Why does s character matter?

50
00:02:13,295 --> 00:02:14,368
Link by link.

51
00:02:14,718 --> 00:02:15,618
Link one.

52
00:02:15,540 --> 00:02:19,367
An s orbital sits closer to
the nucleus than a p orbital.

53
00:02:20,117 --> 00:02:21,017
Link two.

54
00:02:20,832 --> 00:02:25,223
So the more s character, the closer the
bonding electrons stay to the carbon.

55
00:02:25,973 --> 00:02:26,873
Link three.

56
00:02:26,866 --> 00:02:28,361
So bonds get shorter.

57
00:02:28,361 --> 00:02:32,951
Carbon to carbon is one hundred fifty
four picometres in ethane, one hundred

58
00:02:32,951 --> 00:02:36,337
thirty four in ethene, one
hundred twenty in ethyne.

59
00:02:37,087 --> 00:02:37,987
Link four.

60
00:02:37,871 --> 00:02:39,528
Shorter bonds are stronger.

61
00:02:39,528 --> 00:02:43,788
The double bond needs six hundred
eighty one kilojoules per mole to break.

62
00:02:43,788 --> 00:02:46,698
The single bond, only
three hundred forty eight.

63
00:02:47,448 --> 00:02:48,348
Link five.

64
00:02:48,237 --> 00:02:51,404
Electrons held close also
make the carbon pull harder.

65
00:02:51,404 --> 00:02:54,163
So an s p carbon is the
most electronegative.

66
00:02:54,913 --> 00:02:57,791
Now look closely at
the double bond itself.

67
00:02:57,791 --> 00:03:00,099
Ethane and ethene, side by side.

68
00:03:00,449 --> 00:03:04,634
In ethane, the two carbons share
one sigma bond, end to end.

69
00:03:04,634 --> 00:03:07,796
Each end can spin, like
a wheel on its axle.

70
00:03:08,196 --> 00:03:13,260
In ethene, the leftover p orbitals
stand side by side and overlap sideways.

71
00:03:13,260 --> 00:03:15,460
That extra bond is a pi bond.

72
00:03:15,460 --> 00:03:18,085
All six atoms lie flat in one plane.

73
00:03:18,485 --> 00:03:19,643
Pause and think.

74
00:03:19,643 --> 00:03:23,334
Can the two ends of ethene
spin, like the ends of ethane?

75
00:03:26,534 --> 00:03:27,434
No!

76
00:03:26,950 --> 00:03:31,427
A twist turns the p orbitals away from
each other, and the pi bond breaks.

77
00:03:31,427 --> 00:03:34,168
So rotation about a
double bond is restricted.

78
00:03:34,568 --> 00:03:38,880
The pi cloud sits above and
below the plane, out in the open.

79
00:03:38,880 --> 00:03:40,727
Attackers reach it easily.

80
00:03:40,727 --> 00:03:43,543
So pi bonds are the most reactive spots.

81
00:03:44,193 --> 00:03:45,093
Remember this.

82
00:03:45,089 --> 00:03:47,626
Count carbon's bonds,
and you know its shape.

83
00:03:48,326 --> 00:03:49,742
Now, one worked example.

84
00:03:49,742 --> 00:03:53,150
Find each carbon's hybridisation,
and count the sigma and pi bonds.

85
00:03:53,450 --> 00:03:54,350
Step one.

86
00:03:54,224 --> 00:03:56,799
Carbons one and two share a double bond.

87
00:03:56,799 --> 00:04:00,109
Each makes three sigma
bonds, so both are s p two.

88
00:04:00,859 --> 00:04:01,759
Step two.

89
00:04:01,640 --> 00:04:04,383
Carbons three and four
share a triple bond.

90
00:04:04,383 --> 00:04:07,383
Each makes two sigma
bonds, so both are s p.

91
00:04:08,133 --> 00:04:09,093
Step three.

92
00:04:09,093 --> 00:04:10,480
Count sigma bonds.

93
00:04:10,480 --> 00:04:12,461
Every bond has exactly one.

94
00:04:12,461 --> 00:04:15,829
Three carbon to carbon,
four carbon to hydrogen.

95
00:04:15,829 --> 00:04:16,840
Seven sigma.

96
00:04:17,590 --> 00:04:18,509
Step four.

97
00:04:18,509 --> 00:04:19,754
Count pi bonds.

98
00:04:19,754 --> 00:04:23,237
One in the double bond,
two in the triple bond.

99
00:04:23,237 --> 00:04:24,137
Three pi.

100
00:04:24,853 --> 00:04:28,490
So the left end is flat, and the
right end is a straight line.

101
00:04:29,140 --> 00:04:30,602
Now, the exam trap.

102
00:04:30,952 --> 00:04:35,221
Students say a double bond is just
two single bonds, so it must be twice

103
00:04:35,221 --> 00:04:36,121
as strong.

104
00:04:36,420 --> 00:04:37,320
Wrong.

105
00:04:37,000 --> 00:04:39,361
It is one sigma plus one weaker pi.

106
00:04:39,361 --> 00:04:43,426
Three hundred ninety seven plus two
hundred eighty four is six hundred

107
00:04:43,426 --> 00:04:44,326
eighty one.

108
00:04:44,302 --> 00:04:46,938
Less than twice three hundred forty eight.

109
00:04:47,589 --> 00:04:48,838
Where is this asked?

110
00:04:48,838 --> 00:04:52,493
Boards and entrance tests ask for
each carbon's hybridisation, sigma and

111
00:04:52,493 --> 00:04:54,211
pi counts, and bond lengths.

112
00:04:54,811 --> 00:04:55,826
Exam level now.

113
00:04:55,826 --> 00:05:00,064
What is the hybridisation of each
carbon, left to right, in this molecule?

114
00:05:00,064 --> 00:05:01,122
Pause and solve.

115
00:05:06,522 --> 00:05:08,068
The answer is A.

116
00:05:08,068 --> 00:05:11,496
s p two, s p two, s p three, s p.

117
00:05:11,996 --> 00:05:16,361
The first two carbons share a
double bond, so both are s p two.

118
00:05:16,861 --> 00:05:21,638
The middle CH two has four
single bonds, so it is s p three.

119
00:05:22,138 --> 00:05:26,337
The last carbon has a triple
bond to nitrogen, so it is s p.

120
00:05:26,337 --> 00:05:30,116
B traps students who count
four bonds and say s p three.

121
00:05:30,716 --> 00:05:32,256
Here is your recap.

122
00:05:32,256 --> 00:05:33,839
Screenshot this card.

123
00:05:37,039 --> 00:05:39,225
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

124
00:05:39,625 --> 00:05:40,525
One.

125
00:05:40,108 --> 00:05:42,194
Why is carbon called tetravalent?

126
00:05:42,994 --> 00:05:43,894
Two.

127
00:05:43,464 --> 00:05:47,017
Which is shorter, the carbon
bond in ethene or in ethyne?

128
00:05:47,817 --> 00:05:48,717
Three.

129
00:05:48,302 --> 00:05:50,723
Why can't the two ends
of a double bond spin?

130
00:05:51,623 --> 00:05:55,836
Next time: how do chemists draw a big
molecule with just one zigzag line?

131
00:05:55,836 --> 00:05:58,195
We find out in structural representations.
